From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 14:09:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] windows.web: implement the four HttpContent classes.

One body, four ways of giving it: a string, a buffer, a stream, or a list of
other contents.  They are the same object with a different source, so what they
share -- the headers, the length, the identity -- is written once.

A string content knows its own length in the bytes that will travel, which is
UTF-8 and not the UTF-16 the HSTRING holds, so a caller that sets
Content-Length from it sets one it can keep.  A buffer content refuses a window
that runs past the end of its buffer rather than describing a body nobody can
send.  A stream content and a multipart one both decline to state a length:
a stream is as long as it turns out to be, and a multipart body's length
depends on a boundary chosen when it is written.

The factories set Content-Type the way a caller would, through the same header
map, and the encodings this does not write say so rather than quietly producing
UTF-8 under another name.

Reading a body back needs asynchronous operations and stream types this tree
does not have; each of those four says which one.

classes.idl gains all six of the new classes, including the two messages the
last commit added and left out of it: the registration resource is what
regsvr32 writes, and a class missing from it works only where someone wrote the
registry by hand.

tools/winrtprobe checks the length is counted in UTF-8 bytes, that the default
and the explicit media type both land in the header map, that ToString gives a
string content's own text back, that an unimplemented encoding says so, and
that a multipart content refuses a null part.  58 of 58 checks pass against the
prefix Office runs in.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit cd6a6fed2cdb2963f9eb7923578344baede00810)

